#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535} :activity/id #uuid "3903e573-e595-5f4d-8dbd-a1c1b28ab808" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050386 :topic/id #uuid "44bfd7a4-62f3-56cf-89cb-1d3f0e772674" :topic/math-academy-id 2456} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148781 :tutorial/content "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n$0$\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%$.\n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= part \\div percent \\\\\n&= 540 \\div 30% \\\\\n&= 540 \\div \\frac{3}{10} \\\\\n&= 540 \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{540}{3} \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 180 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 1, 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800$." :tutorial/id #uuid "69026176-778a-58ea-8ccf-185968679bd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8170 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e956a0b5-6283-59e2-b00b-6fff43cb6aaf" :step/math-academy-id 72289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147148 :question/id #uuid "e650884c-e858-5213-8ff4-a39f9358edc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13066"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb8b97e4-704c-5b13-97c7-6eb97aca392f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921